An extract from an obituary in The Astrological Journal, Spring 1970.

    I was deeply sieved that my old friend» Cyril Fagan, died on 5 January 1970 of a heart
attack. It was under his instructions that I became interested in the sidereal zodiac for which I
will be eternally grateful. He began his astrological career at the age of 20 in 1916 and mode a
thorough study of all astrological techniques. Few astrologers have been better equipped by a
deep study of the astrology of the masters. Indeed it was impossible to find an astrological topic
which he had not discussed and often argued about with the leading astrologers of the time.
  He had the great advantage of being a first-class mathematician to whom calculations came
easily. His later study of Egyptology fitted him for the study of ancient horoscopes and this led to
his rediscovery of the ancient sidereal zodiac. As he had written to me he had become dissatisfied
with the tropical zodiac and was seeking something better.
 He began his Egyptological study in the middle 3O's and found that the most ancient horoscopes
were all in the sidereal zodiac. During the course of his research he calculated a map for a New
Moon In 786 B.C and found that in this map all planets were In their degrees of exaltation either
in longitude or in heliacal rising. .It was from this discovery that he calculated the ayanasma
(the distance between the two zodiacs) for that date. Statistical research has moved this figure
by only six minutes and this is the ayanasma which we siderealIsts use today.
 Few had the knowledge to argue successfully with him. He was a kindly man who gave freely of
his time in helping students of the sidereal. He wrote easily and fluently with a style which was
all his own.
 His first book entitled Zodiacs Old and Naw was published in 1951. This work is to he
supplemented by a new book dealing' in fuller detail with his astrological discoveries which will
be called Astrological Origins . This book is to be issued by Llewellyn’s of America in March
of this yeari 1970).
 He had made the decision to use the Octoplan house system Invented by Imhotep, an eightfold
house division of the heavens running clockwise as opposed to all other systems which aré
twelve-fold and run counter clockwise. He had previously been a staunch supporter of the
Campanian system.

 One of his latest statements was that he had discovered that PLUTO Is the planet. besides others,
of loneliness. He had it on the midheaven and I think that he was to some extend a lonely man. In
this he was not helped by a serious deafness which tended to cut him off from others. But he was
supreme on paper; this was his real love.

Sidereal astrology has begun to make an impact on astrological thought in America where some
schools devoted to its study hove been opened. It has not made much impact on thought in Europe
or England. It remains for those, like myself, who believe in the sidereal zodiac, to continue
their studies and carry on the work of Cyril Fagan, the father of modern sidereal astrology. By
us he will be sadly missed as he was the source of our inspiration.
