                      A brief resume oj my tife


My mother was born in Johannesburg, S.A. on January 6, 1897 ; ASC Libra.
She died at the age of 72 (stroke on July 15, death on July 31, 1969).
My father, Wladimir (Waldemar), was bom in Kiev, Ukraine on June 22,
1884; ASC Leo With Jupiter rising. He died at the age of 87 in his sleep on
May   15,   1972. It is remarkable that he had no severe illness or any
surgical operation. His costly hobby was breeding Pointers and Setters, for
which he was well-known in England and elsewhere.


My parents moved to Berlin when I was about one year old.
They separated in October 1923.’
Although I was only four years old, I clearly remember that 1 had a very
bad time and suffered very much. 1 lived thereafter with my father and the
loss of my mother caused me psychological problems.


 At.an early age I was fascinated with music. My father frequently took
me to brass band concerts in the Berlin Zoo and also to the famous Café
Vaterland, where I would listen attentively, especially to the leading
violinist. I received my first piano lessons at age five. My father soon
found out that I have perfect pitch, and I began violin lessons the follo­
wing year.


 My father, who was the general representative of firms selling machine
tools, often had to travel abroad, especially to Soviet Russia. He therefore
engaged a governess for me. This was very fortunate, as 1 came to feel to
a certain extent that she replaced my mother.
She was a well-educated Russian emigré, a victim of the Soviet revolution
in 1917. She sang very well and was also able to accompany me on the
piano, which made my first steps with the violin more interesting. At this
time we all - my father, my governess and myself - used to speak Russian,
a language that was to be very helpful to me in my later life and which 1
have preserved until today.
 My progress with the violin was promising but not too exciting.
By the time 1 was ten years old 1 participated in school concerts and at
fourteen, as the result of a competition, I was chosen to play a sonata by
Veracini before an audience of more than a thousand in the main studio of
Berlin Broadcast.
 But my father did not intend that I should become a professional violi­
nist and this, together with circumstances, connected with high school,
sports and other hobbies, prevented me from pursuing the art of the violin
more intensively. Hence the most fruitful years for any active violinist,
those between ten and sixteen, passed; and the result was a violinist who
played reasonably well but with no qualifications to be a “wonder child "
or a notable soloist. My position in the secondary school, which German
children attend from age ten to nineteen, was satisfactory. I was among
the best in class during the first years but later I dropped down to a more
medium level. This was mainly the result of not doing homework, with the
stress of copying, homework before the start of the          lessons. My class
reports showed excellent and good notes in mathematics, French, music,
biology and sports. The general summary in most of my reports: "equally
interested in all subjects."
  My life as a schoolboy was accompanied by much psychological frustra­
tion and suffering. My father’s liaison with the young woman who was my
violin teacher, lasting for six years, brought about serious troubles. She
lived permanently in our house and 1 often felt completely isolated and
neglected by my father.        Marfa Neuss, aged 23, was a very talented
violinist who was regarded as one of the best German violin players.
Unfortunately she did not have the necessary psychological understanding
of a child ; nor, of course, did she have any time for me, being anxious
about her own career as an artist.        \
  It was Maria's interference that lost me my governess. I was then aged
nine. From then on I was practically always on my own with my thoughts
and problems.
  By the time I had started secondary school my father was treating me as
 an adult. This caused me to become completely self-reliant and to develop
 some sort of resistance against my father. The result was psychological
 strength and ambition (SUN,MERCURY,MARS) but also some complexes
 (SATURN). These psychological circumstances combined together to stimu­
 late early maturity and puberty. Already from the age of fourteen I was
 attracted by the fair sex and had permanent girl friends.
     This, of course, without any sexual activity which in those days was
taboo for teenagers. I was also very fond of dancing. Not being under
permanent control, 1 used to attend fancy dress balls in the costume of a
Sicilian fisherman.


 1 graduated on March 12, 1937       and immediately (on April 4) had to begin
my labour service        which brought me to Silesia near the now Polish town
of Sagan. At the start I lived in comparatively primitive barracks with
fourteen people in a room. Each of us had only a small locker in which it
was difficult to arrange all of one's equipment, not to mention personal
belongings. Thé food was bad and the har'd labour tiring and depressing. We
had to cultivate heathland. We had each been given a heavy pickaxe. Each
worker achieved results depending on his own talents, his strength and the
job he had previously. I had no talent for this work and fell below the
minimum. By contrast peasants mastered everything with ease. In later
years I found parallels with this fortunately very short period of my life
in    the   novel   of   Solzhenitzyn,   ‘'One   day   in   the   life   of   Ivan
Denisowich (in a Russian forced-labour camp. )
During the first days of my stay in this camp I had the opportunity to
distinguish myself in a sporting competition, where together with another
student I became joint first of 120, with results I never had achieved
before. The 100 metres I mastered under 12 seconds and threw the shot
put over 10 metres.
 Unfortunately, being six feet one inch in height, on the first day of my
arrival in this camp I was chosen for the annual "Führer Parade" in Nurem­
berg. This would have meant more drill and stress.
     All this happened within the first days. On Sunday April        11, one day
before my eighteenth birthday, I met a musician who was in the band of
the labour service district. When he heard that I played the violin and the
clarinet, he promised to speak with the band leader about a transfer for
me. ;
 Sooner than I had expected I was called to audition. I had some trouble
demonstrating my clarinet playing and only was able to play a C (B) major
scale. My violin playing apparently greatly impressed the “Gruppen Musik­
leiter" and I was accepted. The commanding officer of the labour camp
took special care to equip me with a completely new outfit
 At the end of April I joined the band       What a contrast and a release !
Our band was completely on its own in an old saw-mill, a little outside
of Sagan (Silesia).
      It was really a comfortable, quite lazy living. Three hours of rehearsals,
     two hours of single exercises, then for me, with the lowest rank of
     "Arbeitsmann“ (labour man), a little bit of cleaning the barracks. On
     weekends 1 used to play light and dance music as a band leader in Sagan
     and in the surrounding villages. On Mondays, our free day, 1 sometimes tra­
     velled home to Berlin by express train as the railway station was only
     some hundred yards away from our barracks.
       1 was able to afford this luxury and other comforts as well , because my
     private income as a violinist was enough for my needs.
       A few weeks before the end of my labour service the music band was
     dissolved and I had to work very hard as a harvest volunteer on a farm.
     ’ I was discharged on November 1 5. After a break of only ten days 1 was
0'^^ conscripted ®.


      Originally 1 tried to start my two years military service in an Infantry
      regiment stationed near my home in Berlin. However, on the advice of a
      friend of my father, I joined the famous regiment of guards in Berlin
      named after the Minister of War, General Hermann Goering.
      This was no longer a regiment but already rather a division with many
      different branches, such as an elite cavalry squadron consisting of only
      fifty people which I was able to join by recommendation. 1 soon found out
      this was a great mistake, because the physical demands exceeded the
      strength of an 18-year-old youngster. Our duty began at 5:30 in the mor­
      ning and ended with theoretical instructions at eight at night. Most of the
      other new volunteers were already experienced in the treatment and riding
      of horses. Some complexes naturally crept in and worsened the situation.
      Only in infantry exercises, in sport, including shooting, and especially in
      the theoretical training was 1 able to challenge the farmers.
       When the other sections of this so-called regiment ended their weekly
      service at noon on Saturday, the young volunteers of the cavalry squad,
      including myself, had to clean the rooms and stables. We sometimes
      worked until six in the evening. But this was not all.
        Our leave finished at 2 Sunday morning yet the recruits of the other
      sections had a continuous leave until Sunday night. Every Sunday morning,
      from seven-thirty on, 1 had to groom two horses, leaving the barracks for
      a short break at noon.                   i
      No wonder that on the 13th of January 1938, after an unassuming birthday
      celebration for our patron Hermann Goering, I became sick with jaun­
      dice^. This caused a welcome break for a fortnight in a very comfortable
      military hospital.
I was certainly not willing to handle this horse business for the rest of my
two years military service. Here my music helped again. A very fortunate
audition for the monstrous music band of the regiment, consisting of
sixty-five members, allowed me to leave the cavalry squad at the end of
February 1938 ®.


By March 12 I was on the way to Austria with some units of the regiment.
We were to take part in the infamous Anschluss. Ata Hitler Parade in
Vienna in March, where our band played/ Adolf Hitler solemnly declared
that there would be no more territorial claims against any European
country. At the time this impressed me very much, because it would mean
that we need not expect more international trouble from Hitler's lust for
power. As we all know. Hitler broke this promise in September 1938 when
the German armies ocupied parts of Czechoslovakia.


My time with the brass band was not at all successful. I occasionally had
to entertain the officers and their wives with light and dance music at
balls and other festivities, leading a band of twelve musicians.
  My clarinet playing did not improve, because I did not practice. For this
reason I did not have a good reputation with the "Stabsmusikmeister"
(military band leader). On the other hand he knew that I would only stay for
another year in the band, because I was not one of the professional sol­
diers who served for at least twelve years. Fate knew otherwise. I was
*®
 transferred  on the 1 st of November 1938 to an airforce unit where they
badly needed a leading violinist. As a consolation I was promoted         to
corporal;


Earlier, in May of the same year, I had fallen in love   with the 17-year-
old daughter of our sergeant major and deputy band leader. Here things
went very well as the deputy, by the way an excellent clarinet player, was
very fond of me and had no objections to our liaison. This lasted for almost
two years. I had my first sexual experiences with this very pretty girl in
1938.’


My new garrison was an anti-aircraft unit in Mecklenburg , a province in
the north of Germany. There I had the opportunity to develop my abilities
as a violinist. Our military band master was a devotee of string music . His
name was NOTE (note in German and English). Here we may say         "Nomen
est omen". His original ambition had been to become the conductor of an
operetta orchestra. He had not succeeded in this, but as a military band
leader he tried to cultivate more string music..
  At rehearsals we usually prepared concerts of easy symphonies, opera
and operetta music while the true function of a military band, the brass
music, was neglected.
  Concert tours for the band as a light orchestra were arranged in autumn
and winter to many places. This took place under the slogan " Winter-
hilfskonzerte" which translated means, concerts in winter for the bene­
fit of the poor and unemployed. In towns we produced concerts of light
music, while in villages, after the official concert, there was dancing
until late in the night. As we were lucky enough to have plenty of good
musicians, this dance music was performed by several groups in rotation,
giving the musicians a chance to participate in the dancing. 1 also used to
lead a group of four to eight musicians in    cafés at the seaside resort,
'Kuehlungsborn' in Mecklenburg, now playing the violin as well as the
saxophone.
  Although I was rarely able to travel home to Berlin, I was quite happy
with my military service at this time, i was financially independent, even
though the pay of a rank and file soldier was very poor. Indeed, the large
amount of extra Income earned by most of the musicians also caused some
jealousy among the other soldiers who where living In the same barracks.
  Here it should be explained that the English expression “barracks“ for the
buildings where German soldiers were accomodated at the time does not
suit reality at all.
 One of the few useful acts of Adolf Hitler's was to create dwellings
for military personnel which were like comfortable hospitals. This was
especially so for buildings of the Luftwaffe . There were never rooms
where more than four rank and file soldiers were accomodated. Each
sergeant had a room for himself.    All the other comforts, such as dining
halls, swimming pools and sanitary arrangements, were exemplary com­
pared to the accomodations of other European military personnel at that
time.
 From my stay at that garrison (November 1938 - May 1941) I remember
                    *^
one very unpleasant incident   during Christmas 1939. On that day I was
ordered to play dance music wTtHmy^nd at a little nearby village

 inhabited by rather backward people. At the village inn one of our ser­
geants had played primitive country reels and other oldies for months I
had to replace him because he was ill. Naturally I was not prepared for
 this sort of dance music and soon the tipsy peasants began to complain.
  One middle- aged man came up to me during an interval and told me in his
 dialect, which I could hardly understand" You don't play the pieces we
 are looking for'. I could see by his behaviour that he was completely
 drunk. I don't remember my reply, but about a minute later he tried to
 attack me with a chair. It was no major accomplishment to knock out this
 drunk with some well-aimed blows to his chin.
   When the son saw his father lying on the floor he came to his assistance
 and another fight began. The son, obviously with the same habits as his
, father,'was also drunk. I was able to hold him down with a firm grip, not
 intending to harm him any further. Then friends of this young man and
 many others in the crowd came to his assistance, and a real fight began.
 Naturally I was unable to hold my own against such odds.
  Fortunately two brave comrades and two soldiers in uniform came to my
 assistance. 1 was able to escape through a back door without any injury
 other than a slightly swollen face. Music and dancing came to an end. The
 next day I submitted a written report of this incident to my superiors. The
 young man who started the fight by coming to his father’s rescue, original­
 ly classified as an indispensable factory worker, was immediately called
 to arms.
   The war became more evident as France was overrun and occupied. I con­
 tinued to serve well protected behind the scene at the same place with the
 same tasks.
  As I would not agree to bind myself for twelve years in order to become a
 professional soldier and with this to become a sergeant, I decided to re­
 quest a transfer. It was very depressing indeed to see soldiers who had
 signed for twelve years become promoted, while I myself still remained a
 corporal with fewer privileges. It was also very likely that the music band
 would be dissolved and, I must frankly admit, I was not prepared to die or
 to be crippled for Hitler’s madness.
   My perfect knowledge of the Russian language helped me to realize my
 aims. When I was on leave in Berlin a friend of my father in the “Reichs-
 luftfahrtministerium" (Ministry of Aviation) organized examinations for
 me in French and Russian. I failed the French, there having obviously been
 enough candidates, while the examination in Russian consisted      of only a
 short interview.
   Almost a full year passed before I was suddenly informed that I was to
 be transferred to an interpreter unit of the Luftwaffe in May 1941 ’2’. <
   My new garrison was near Potsdam, the former residence of the Prussian
 kings, only half an hour away from my home in Berlin. I was soon able to
 arrange to live in my father’s flat and to go daily to work at office times.
 In the severe circumstances of war time in Germany, my working hours
 from nine in the morning until five o’clock in the afternoon were unusually
 moderate. At first I had a very dull job. It consisted of locating and
 extracting important points such as villages, bridges, mills etc. with
 coordinates from original Russian staff maps.
    Later, after a second short examination, 1 was tranferred to the deci­
 phering department. This job attracted me much more, not least because I
 became quite successful at it. At the start the deciphering tasks I was
 given were comparatively simple tables, ten by ten = hundred positions.The
 ciphers were given in two digits.
These tables which still exist in a similar form in some countries are con-




These tables are encoded daily by changing numerical rows and columns. In
my example they are as follows:


Hor   2156890437
 Vert: 48 5 1 79063 2
The letter A will be 24; T = 12 ; Semicolon = 53, etc.


  This primitive method of cipher is very easy to decipher, whether the
underlying structure of the table is known or not. It is always more a
matter of speed, i.e. to crack it in the shortest possible time. The inter­
national name for this cypher is CESAR, after the one who is said to have
first used this form of covert communication.
    Two days before the Invasion of Russia our unit belonging to the ObdL,
Oberkommando der Luftwaffe (High Command of the Airforce, Headquar­
ters), was transferred by air to the region of Johannesburg, East Prus­
sia’^, where we lived in a youth hostel right on the shore of a beautiful
large lake. When the war with Russia began we first had a very tiring and
stressful time, working 12 hours a day in six-hour shifts. Huge quantities
of Russian wireless messages were being picked up, evidence       of anxiety
and desperation. Even those who, like myself, didn’t believe in Hitler's fi­
nal victory came to the conclusion that Russian resistance could only last
at best for a couple of weeks. By then about eight thousand planes and
three Russian armies had been almost destroyed.
August 1, 1941, I became a sergeant’"’, a memorable day, because it was
the only time in my life that I got completely drunk. A make-shift canteen
had been erected, actually only a board fixed between two trees.
I invited my comrades in separate groups to hard drinks. With each group I
took at least one "Korn",   a schnaps made from wheat. I must have had a
good dozen altogether. Still I was quite firm on my feet. However when the
