West Berlin, to get some money from the government failed. At that time
Berlin received very few subsidies from West Germany. An invitation from
the East German and Russian authorities to take the orchestra over into
East.Berlin with the full pay was considered, but we rejected this for
many reasons.
 Our problems were eased to some extent, when an obscure US recording
company. Remington, signed a contract with us. This covered about thirty
percent of our budget. They promptly paid for our recordings, but two years
later all their recorded material was confiscated and sold to a German
firm because Remington had illegally made use of hlarshall Plan money.
The wages of our orchestra members had to be reduced considerably. With
the money we still received from RIAS, Remington and Deutsche Grammo­
phone we just managed to keep the orchestra together.
Now a Society, we feared that prominent members would look for other
jobs. Fortunately this only hajjpened in a few cases and the standard of the
orchestra was maintained.
  1 did not stand for the October 1954 committee elections because, with
four other members, I had accepted a job with the Radio Symphony Or­
chestra in Dublin. I arrived there on February 2, 1955
      In order to support a family of four persons, I decided it was too risky
to stay with the orchestra society, which now called itself RSO (Radio
Symphony Orchestral.This orchestra still survives, while Ferenc Friscay,
