  In Dublin I had the opportunity to play with several chamber music groups
such as the Dublin Chamber orchestra and with a small group of four
members,      the Dublin Harpsichord Ensemble. These regular perfor­
mances were vital for me, because I always found that playing only in a
symphony orchestra did not provide the psychological balance I needed.
 Besides my performing I taught violin in two Catholic schools. We stayed
in our own house: life in Ireland was not too bad. In some ways this was
my most happy time, although the standard of living in Ireland fell back
from year to year by comparison with the West German "Wirtschafts-
wunder" (miracle of economic development).
      During my stay in Dublin I was frequently approached by former col­
